---
/**
 * The differentiator page.
 *
 * This is the one argument the product has that a hosted CRM cannot answer, so it gets a
 * page of its own - and it includes the case against, because a page that only argues one
 * way is read as marketing and discounted.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
	title="Why self hosted — Ultimate Client Manager"
	description="What you get by running your CRM on your own hosting, what it costs you in effort, and who should not do it."
	current="/self-hosted/"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<p class="kicker">The honest version</p>
			<h1>Why run it yourself</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Self hosting is a trade, not a free lunch. Here is what you get for it and what
				it costs you, including the cases where you should pick a subscription instead.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<h2>What you get</h2>

			<h3>The data is in your database</h3>
			<p>
				Everything the application stores is in MySQL tables on your own hosting. You can
				back it up on your own schedule, query it with anything that speaks SQL, and move
				it elsewhere without asking permission or waiting for an export queue.
			</p>

			<h3>The price does not grow with the team</h3>
			<p>
				There is no per seat charge, because there is nobody to charge it. Add as many
				staff accounts as you need. The cost of the software was the cost of the software.
			</p>

			<h3>You have the source</h3>
			<p>
				The whole application is PHP you can read. Each feature is a plugin, and the
				<a href="/docs/developers/customising/">custom folder</a> lets you override almost
				any screen, template or query without editing the files you were shipped — so your
				changes survive the next update instead of being lost to it.
			</p>

			<h3>It does not phone home</h3>
			<p>
				The application contacts no outside service to function. The update check and the
				help panel are the only outbound requests it makes, both can be turned off, and the
				<a href="/docs/using/help-system/">help system page</a> sets out exactly what the
				help request carries and what it does not. There is no analytics, no telemetry and
				no licence check.
			</p>

			<h3>It keeps working</h3>
			<p>
				A subscription ending is a deadline. An installed application that is already
				running on your server is not. If this project stopped tomorrow your installation
				would carry on doing what it does.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="tint">
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<h2>What it costs you</h2>
			<p>
				All of this is real work that somebody has to do, and if nobody does it you end up
				with an out of date installation holding your customer list. Worth being clear
				about before you buy.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Hosting.</strong> You need an account that meets the
					<a href="/requirements/">requirements</a> — in particular PHP 8.4, which not
					every host offers by default yet.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Updates.</strong> They are not automatic in the sense a hosted service
					is automatic. The application checks for them and installs them when you say so,
					and somebody has to say so.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Backups.</strong> There is a backup feature, but it is your job to run
					it, and to check that what it produced can actually be restored.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Email deliverability.</strong> Invoices and newsletters go out from your
					server or your SMTP provider. Getting that reliably into inboxes is your
					problem, not the software's.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Security patches.</strong> You apply them. The project publishes them;
					an installation nobody updates does not benefit from that.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<div class="note">
				<p>
					<strong>Pick a hosted CRM instead if</strong> nobody at your firm is comfortable
					with a hosting control panel, or if you would rather pay monthly than be
					responsible for backups and updates. That is a reasonable choice and this is the
					wrong product for it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<h2>Still interested?</h2>
			<p>
				The <a href="/docs/install/requirements/">requirements page</a> is the one to read
				before spending anything — it is specific about PHP versions and extensions, and it
				is the thing that catches people out.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a class="button" href="https://codecanyon.net/item/freelance-project-invoice-crm/2621629">
					Buy on CodeCanyon
				</a>
				<a class="button quiet" href="/docs/">Read the documentation</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
